<p>Meridial is a Focused Research Organisation (FRO) with a mission to bring connectomics into the living brain. We are building a platform for longitudinal, synapse-resolution mapping of functional neural circuitry in preclinical models. Our aim is to deploy this platform for translational projects and improve drug development outcomes for disorders of the central nervous system.</p>
<p>Meridial is a new not-for-profit FRO in the Convergent Research&nbsp;<a href="https://www.convergentresearch.org/ecosystem">Ecosystem</a> and funded by the Advanced Research and Invention Agency (<a href="https://aria.org.uk/">ARIA</a>).</p>
<h3>The technical challenge</h3>
<p>Our imaging platform records the activity of neurons and synapses in the living brain. The scientific value of these recordings depends on converting raw images into reliable, quantitative data.</p>
<p>This is a demanding image processing problem. Fluorescence signals are faint, noisy and spatially overlapping; residual tissue motion and other imaging artefacts must be corrected; in addition to conventional planar frames, data may be acquired from arbitrarily shaped 3D regions or several depths at once; properties vary between signal types and experimental configurations; and the same cells and synapses must be reliably matched across imaging sessions spanning weeks. You will build a software package that addresses these challenges in two modes: real-time processing and visualisation, with latencies of tens to hundreds of milliseconds, that inform experimental decisions at the microscope, and offline pipelines that deliver maximum accuracy for scientific analysis and serve as the benchmark for the real-time methods.</p>
<p>The impact of this work is direct and visible: your software will be used at the microscope as experiments unfold, and your pipelines will generate the data underpinning new insights into how neural circuits function and how they change in disease.</p><br><strong>Key responsibilities</strong><br><div>
<p>You will own Meridial’s image processing software end to end, starting from the interface with microscope control and acquisition all the way to processed results prepared for biological analysis. This is a role with a high degree of autonomy: you will make the main design and technology decisions, set priorities with the people who use the software, and work out what to build as requirements emerge.</p>
<h4>Who you will work with</h4>

<li><strong>Experimental team.</strong> The neuroscientists running experiments are the main users of the real-time tools. You will work with them at the microscope, understand their problems and turn joint solutions into reliable tools.</li>
<li><strong>Senior Software Engineer. </strong>Develops the microscope control and image acquisition software, mostly in Rust. Your real-time code exchanges data with that software but need not be part of it. Together, you will decide how the two systems connect.</li>
<li><strong>Neuroscience Data Scientist.</strong> Owns Meridial’s database and the downstream scientific analysis. You will ensure processed results reach the database with a full record of how they were produced.</li>
<li><strong>Reporting line.</strong> You will join the Computational Team and report to its Head once appointed, and to the Director of Experimental Research until then.</li>

<h4>What you will do</h4>

<li>Build robust pipelines for motion and artefact correction, segmentation of cells and synapses, separation of overlapping signals and extraction of activity over time, adapted to different signal types.</li>
<li>Register data from flexible 3D imaging modes to reference volumes, and build tools that find the same field of view and match cells and synapses across sessions, both live at the microscope and offline at high resolution.</li>
<li>Develop real-time versions of these methods that keep up with streaming data, including fast identification of cells as stimulation targets.</li>
<li>Build live visualisations and summary measures, such as rolling averages, response histograms and noise metrics, that help scientists steer experiments.</li>
<li>Define, with the Senior Software Engineer, the interface between acquisition and processing, with a focus on throughput and low latency.</li>
<li>Evaluate and adapt existing open-source tools, develop new methods where needed, and validate them against ground truth, using offline results as the benchmark for the real-time processing.</li>
<li>Optimise for speed and scale, using multi-threading, GPUs or compiled languages where needed, so that processing keeps up with large and growing datasets.</li>
<li>Write clean, tested and documented code, and set up version control, continuous integration and documentation practices for the codebase.</li>
<li>Use AI coding assistants to work efficiently, while keeping code correct and maintainable.</li>
<li>Plan and track work in Jira or another project management system, in short iterations with the experimental team.</li>
<li>Turn prototypes into reproducible tools that scientists can use and that can be released as open source.</li>

</div><br><strong>The person we are looking for</strong><br><div>
<p>You are an experienced software engineer with a background in image analysis or computer vision. You enjoy hard technical problems and set high standards for correctness, performance and code quality. You like working directly with the people who use your software: understanding their problems, trying out ideas with them and turning the best into reliable tools.</p>
<p>You enjoy building from scratch and are comfortable with the ambiguity of an early-stage organisation. As the main developer of our image processing software, you will work independently and shape how it evolves.</p>
<p>Experience in any of these areas would help you hit the ground running, but none is required: real-time or streaming data processing, microscopy or scientific image analysis, statistics, or machine learning for image analysis. A background in biology, biophysics or neuroscience would also help.</p>
</div><br><strong>Essential experience</strong><br><div>

<li>Experience with image analysis or computer vision, such as segmentation, motion correction, registration or denoising, including image quality measures like signal-to-noise ratio.</li>
<li>5+ years of software development experience, in industry or research (for example, PhD or postdoctoral work where software was a main output).</li>
<li>Strong proficiency in Python, with good engineering practice: Git, automated testing and well-documented code.</li>
<li>Experience with performance optimisation, such as multi-threading or GPU programming, and with a compiled language such as C/C++ or Rust (if not Rust, a willingness to learn it).</li>
<li>Experience building interactive, high-performance data visualisations.</li>
<li>Experience building data-intensive pipelines for large datasets, from requirements through to deployment.</li>
<li>Experience working directly with end users who are not developers, gathering requirements and turning feedback into improvements.</li>
<li>Hands-on experience using AI coding assistants in your day-to-day development.</li>

</div><br><strong>Physical demands</strong><br><div>

<li>Fine motor skills for operating computers and office/lab equipment</li>
<li>Ability to move light-to-moderately heavy items (typical lab supplies/equipment); may include occasional lifting/carrying with appropriate equipment (e.g., carts/dollies)</li>

