osition, budget allocations and the technology roadmap amongst others.</p><p>Reporting to our CTO, you look two to five years ahead and beyond, and shape our product line across several generations, so that what we build today still makes sense for our customers tomorrow. You work with our customers' technology roadmaps, marketing, the heads of R&amp;D, our function architects and customer support, and you lead through persuasion rather than formal authority. Our system engineer builds on your concept and makes the current program work to spec. The role includes occasional travel to customers in Asia, the US and Europe.</p><h2><strong><span style="color:#1F4E79">Key Responsibilities</span></strong></h2><ul><li><p>Own the system architecture of our metrology product line, looking two to five years ahead and beyond, across several product generations</p></li><li><p>Make and justify the key concept choices, weighing physics, performance, cost and risk</p></li><li><p>Decompose the system into modules and interfaces, and allocate the key budgets, such as throughput, cost of goods, availability and performance</p></li><li><p>Translate our customers' technology roadmaps and future needs into product requirements, together with marketing and customers</p></li><li><p>Define our platform and reuse strategy, and decide which technologies we invest in</p></li><li><p>Align the heads of R&amp;D and our function architects behind the architecture, and take on adjacent responsibilities as the company grows, such as representing Invisix in technical discussions with customers and partners</p></li></ul><h2><strong><span style="color:#1F4E79">Is this you?</span></strong></h2><ul><li><p>MSc or PhD in physics, applied physics, electrical or mechanical engineering or a related field, with at least 10 years of experience developing complex high-tech equipment, including a role as system or product architect</p></li><li><p>Experience in semiconductor equipment, for example lithography, metrology or inspection, or in comparable high-tech equipment, ideally with a product that made it to customers</p></li><li><p>Strong physics depth, in optics or measurement physics, so you can judge concept choices on their physical merits</p></li><li><p>Experience making architecture trade-offs across mechanics, electronics, optics and software, and setting system budgets for performance, throughput and cost</p></li><li><p>A preference for breadth over depth, comfort with ambiguity, the ability to make sound decisions on incomplete data, and the skill to persuade without formal authority</p></li><li><p>Active use of AI tooling in your daily work (concept studies, trade-off analysis, documentation) to boost efficiency</p></li></ul><p><strong>Nice to have</strong></p><ul><li><p>Experience working with the technology roadmaps of leading semiconductor manufacturers</p></li><li><p>Experience defining a product platform or reuse strategy across several generations</p></li><li><p>Experience balancing cost of goods and cost of service against performance</p></li><li><p>Experience in a startup or scale-up</p></li></ul><h2><strong><span style="color:#1F4E79">What We Offer</span></strong></h2><ul><li><p>A role at the heart of Invisix's product, shaping what our metrology equipment will be over several generations</p></li><li><p>Competitive salary and benefits, including an Employee Share Ownership Plan</p></li><li><p>A learning and development budget to support your growth</p></li><li><p>Flexible working arrangements, and room to grow into adjacent responsibilities as the team scales</p></li></ul><h2><strong><span style="color:#1F4E79">Interested?</span></strong></h2><p>If this sounds like your kind of challenge, we'd like to hear from you, even if you only recognize yourself in part of this description. Submit your resume, and let's find out together if there's a match.</p><p>If this isn't quite the right position for you but you're still interested in Invisix, reach out to us